  "commit": "aeccb3060d6546b294c92b063434b17ddf07064d",
  "description": "Opens on a main that two sessions are now writing to. PR #163, a design system and a rebuilt file browser, merged from elsewhere while run/0019's last item was in CI, and the collision was not theoretical: it moved the Settings entry out of the navbar into the account dropdown, so resolving the conflict by taking main's side wholesale would have silently dropped item/admin-users's own fix and the PR would still have looked correct. Recorded in decision/0047. tests and assertions are -1 rather than copied from run/0019, per decision/0044: a number carried forward reads as a measurement. item/ledger-field-audit decides whether -1 gains that meaning formally.\n\nTHREE MAIN PUSHES RECORDED LATE, and two of them carry pullRequest: null for a reason worth stating. 95b49d2 is this run's own previous ledger merge, null by the convention every ledger PR follows. c930b60 is PR #176 -- file preview, a command palette and search snippets -- authored in the owner's own session and merged by someone other than this loop; e702176 is PR #272, this loop's fix to a defect in that code. NEITHER CAN CARRY A PR NODE, because a node's implements must name an item and no item covers the preview surface at all. That is not a recording problem to work around by inventing an item: it is the ledger saying plainly that main receives work the item model does not describe, so \"66 of 89\" counts a plan narrower than the product, and item/v1-audit would audit the narrower thing. Raised with the owner rather than resolved here, because the choice -- retroactive items, a decision bounding what the ledger tracks, or a forward item bringing the surface under audit -- changes what the count means.\n\nA LATER LEG, 2026-09-19 evening, opened by re-reading \"the loop has no v1 work it can legitimately perform\" rather than repeating it. That claim had been reported hourly and was wrong in the same shape decision/0099 records: three v1 items ended at something the owner must authorise, and that was read as nothing being buildable. What was actually buildable was a defect: `grep -n palette .github/scripts/container-smoke.mjs` returned nothing. item/palette-smoke, mutation/0032, PRs #277 and #280.\n\nTHE THREE MERGES RECORDED HERE include one, 039cacf, that was a main push from the previous leg and went unrecorded at the time -- the same lapse item/ledger-main-push-record exists to catch, and it was caught here by reading `git log --first-parent` rather than by a red check. That claim was FIRST WRITTEN HERE AS: \"`validate ledger` was GREEN on main at f8d7958 with two merges unrecorded, so the history check does not currently fail on an omission.\" IT IS WRONG, and the correction is the more useful fact. The check does catch an omission -- it caught this run's own next one, refusing 59dae8f with \"is on main ... but does not appear in any run's merges, and a LATER commit does -- so this is a merge nobody wrote down, not one not yet recorded.\" What it cannot catch is a gap at the END of history, because a merge with nothing recorded after it is indistinguishable from one not recorded YET. So the guard is sound and has exactly one blind spot: the most recent unrecorded merges, which is precisely the state a run is in while it is still working. Left as it is, deliberately: closing it would mean failing on every merge between the push and the ledger commit that records it.\n\nWHAT #280 CORRECTED IS THE LEG'S OWN OUTPUT, not someone else's. A consultation checked #277 against the runs it cited, the way decision/0080 says a citation is checked, and found that its docblock described a mutation nobody ran and that its Enter assertion accepted any file preview URL. Both are recorded as decisions 0100 and 0101 rather than quietly patched, because both were written by the same process, in the same hours, that was citing the rules they broke.\nCLOSED 2026-09-20T00:12:00Z, and the closing is itself the finding. This run opened 2026-09-18T08:36:30Z and stayed `active` for about 38 hours carrying 82 merges. Runs 0000 to 0019 lasted between twenty minutes and roughly four hours and carried between zero and seventeen merges each; run/0018's seventeen over 4h14m was the previous outlier. So this run is larger than runs 0011 to 0019 combined.\n\nLOOP.md says \"write docs/ledger/runs/NNNN.jsonld for the iteration\" and uses the word \"iteration\" throughout; the validator's own docblock says \"'active' is the run currently being worked\". Neither describes what this became. The drift shows in this description, which invented \"A LATER LEG, 2026-09-19 evening\" -- an informal unit written in prose because the vocabulary that was supposed to carry it was not being used. Nor does a Run mean \"a session\": runs 0016 to 0019 share this run's agent and were each closed per iteration.\n\nWHAT IT COST, beyond tidiness. Three validator rules weaken while a run stays open: the check that a Decision's or PullRequest's dateCreated falls inside its run's [startTime, endTime] cannot fail against an open-ended window, and two thresholds (MUTATION_RULES_EFFECTIVE_AFTER_RUN, MERGES_OUTCOME_RULE_EFFECTIVE_AFTER_RUN) are denominated in runs, so a 38-hour run is an exemption-shaped window even though nobody used it as one. run/0019's own description says \"the run boundary is not a place to buy an exemption\"; leaving a boundary unclosed is the passive form of the same thing.\n\nThis description was also rewritten rather than appended to on several occasions, which is inside the letter of append-only-for-history only because an active run is not yet history. Closing it ends that too.\nAND A RUN IS WRITTEN FOR AN ITERATION, NOT OPENED BEFORE ONE -- which this commit learned by getting it wrong. The first attempt closed run/0020 and opened an empty run/0021 in the same breath, on the assumption that \"active\" means a run you start and then fill. `validate ledger` refused it:\n\n  PHP Fatal error: Uncaught RuntimeException: witness setup: newest run\n  has no merges to anchor a history: witness on.\n\nThe history witness (LedgerRuleWitnesses, the 'history-unrecorded-interior-merge' case) anchors on the newest run's LAST recorded mergeSha, and the validator separately requires latestRun to be the highest-numbered run. Together those make an empty newest run unrepresentable. That is not an accident of the harness: it encodes the convention LOOP.md step 8 already states -- \"write docs/ledger/runs/NNNN.jsonld for the iteration\" -- where the file DESCRIBES an iteration that happened, merges included, rather than being opened in advance of one. Every run from 0000 to 0019 has merges; none was ever empty.\n\nSo run/0021 is not created here. It will be written by the commit that has something to put in it, and latestRun stays run/0020, now completed. There is no active run at this moment, and that is the honest state rather than a gap.\n\nWORTH RECORDING SEPARATELY: this was caught by CI and not by the local check, because `validate ledger` runs the validator TWICE -- once with a MAIN_SHA for the history comparison, and once with --witnesses -- and only the first was being run here. The second is the one that proves every rule still has something that trips it. A local check that runs half of what CI runs is a local check that can say sound about a tree CI will refuse; both forms are run from now on.",
